                "content": "# Wave Scattering from a Mass-Spring on a String\n\n## Part (1): Equations for $r$ and $t$\n\n**Continuity of displacement at $x = 0$.** Matching the complex amplitudes at $x=0$:\n\n$$y(0^-,t) = \\operatorname{Re}\\!\\big[(1+r)e^{-i\\omega t}\\big], \\qquad y(0^+,t) = \\operatorname{Re}\\!\\big[t\\,e^{-i\\omega t}\\big]$$\n\nEquality for all $t$ requires\n\n$$\\boxed{1 + r = t} \\qquad \\text{(Eq. I)}$$\n\n**Equation of motion of the point mass.** The vertical forces on the mass are:\n\n| Source | Vertical force |\n|--------|---------------|\n| Left string (tension pulls toward $-x$) | $-T\\,\\partial y/\\partial x\\big|_{0^-}$ |\n| Right string (tension pulls toward $+x$) | $+T\\,\\partial y/\\partial x\\big|_{0^+}$ |\n| Spring | $-K\\,y(0,t)$ |\n\nThus\n\n$$M\\,\\ddot{y}(0,t) = T\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial x}\\bigg|_{0^+} - T\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial x}\\bigg|_{0^-} - K\\,y(0,t)$$\n\nComputing the slopes from the ansatz:\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial x}\\bigg|_{0^-} = \\operatorname{Re}\\!\\big[ik(1-r)\\,e^{-i\\omega t}\\big], \\qquad \\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial x}\\bigg|_{0^+} = \\operatorname{Re}\\!\\big[ik\\,t\\,e^{-i\\omega t}\\big]$$\n\nSubstituting the complex amplitudes (and using $-M\\omega^2$ for the acceleration):\n\n$$-M\\omega^2(1+r) = ikT\\,t - ikT(1-r) - K(1+r) \\qquad \\text{(Eq. II)}$$\n\nEquations (I) and (II) are the two equations governing $r$ and $t$.\n\n## Part (2): Solution in terms of $\\eta$\n\nSubstituting $t = 1+r$ into (II):\n\n$$-M\\omega^2(1+r) = ikT(1+r) - ikT(1-r) - K(1+r)$$\n\n$$-M\\omega^2(1+r) = 2ikTr - K(1+r)$$\n\n$$(K - M\\omega^2)(1+r) = 2ikTr$$\n\n$$(K - M\\omega^2) = r\\bigl[2ikT - (K - M\\omega^2)\\bigr] = r\\bigl[(M\\omega^2 - K) + 2ikT\\bigr]$$\n\nNow introducing $\\eta = \\dfrac{K - M\\omega^2}{2kT}$, so that $K - M\\omega^2 = 2kT\\,\\eta$:\n\n$$r = \\frac{2kT\\,\\eta}{-2kT\\,\\eta + 2ikT} = \\frac{\\eta}{i - \\eta}$$\n\n$$t = 1 + r = \\frac{i - \\eta + \\eta}{i - \\eta} = \\frac{i}{i - \\eta}$$\n\n$$\\boxed{r = \\frac{\\eta}{i - \\eta}, \\qquad t = \\frac{i}{i - \\eta}}$$\n\n## Part (3): Energy Reflection and Transmission Coefficients\n\nThe time-averaged power carried by a travelling wave of amplitude $A$ on a string is\n\n$$\\langle P\\rangle = \\frac{1}{2}\\,T\\,\\omega k\\,|A|^2 = \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\mu\\,\\omega^2\\,|A|^2$$\n\nSince all waves here share the same $T$, $\\omega$, and $k$, the power is proportional to $|A|^2$. Therefore:\n\n$$R = \\frac{P_{\\text{ref}}}{P_{\\text{inc}}} = |r|^2, \\qquad \\mathcal{T} = \\frac{P_{\\text{trans}}}{P_{\\text{inc}}} = |t|^2$$\n\nComputing the magnitudes:\n\n$$|r|^2 = \\frac{\\eta^2}{(i-\\eta)(-i-\\eta)} = \\frac{\\eta^2}{\\eta^2 + 1}$$\n\n$$|t|^2 = \\frac{i(-i)}{(i-\\eta)(-i-\\eta)} = \\frac{1}{\\eta^2 + 1}$$\n\nHence\n\n$$\\boxed{R = \\frac{\\eta^2}{1+\\eta^2}, \\qquad \\mathcal{T} = \\frac{1}{1+\\eta^2}}$$\n\nand immediately\n\n$$R + \\mathcal{T} = \\frac{\\eta^2 + 1}{1+\\eta^2} = 1 \\qquad \\checkmark$$\n\nThis confirms energy conservation: with no dissipation, all incident energy is either reflected or transmitted.\n\n## Part (4): Perfect Transmission\n\nPerfect transmission requires $R = 0$, i.e. $\\eta = 0$:\n\n$$K - M\\omega^2 = 0 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\boxed{\\omega = \\sqrt{\\frac{K}{M}}}$$\n\n**Physical explanation.** This is precisely the natural frequency of the mass\u2013spring oscillator. At this frequency the spring force $-Ky$ exactly supplies the force needed to accelerate the mass ($M\\ddot{y} = -M\\omega^2 y = -Ky$), so the **net force the string must exert on the mass is zero**. Equivalently, the discontinuity in slope that the mass would normally impose on the string vanishes. The wave therefore \"sees\" no impedance mismatch and passes through as if the mass were absent ($r=0$, $t=1$).",
                "reasoning_content": "Let me work through this problem step by step.\n\n**Setting up the problem:**\n\nWe have a string on $-\\infty < x < \\infty$ with tension $T$ and linear mass density $\\mu$. At $x=0$, there's a point mass $M$ connected to a fixed support by a spring of constant $K$.\n\nThe wave speed is $c = \\sqrt{T/\\mu}$ and wavenumber $k = \\omega/c$.\n\nThe displacement is:\n$$y(x,t) = \\text{Re}\\left[(e^{ikx} + r e^{-ikx})e^{-i\\omega t}\\right] \\quad \\text{for } x < 0$$\n$$y(x,t) = \\text{Re}\\left[t e^{ikx}e^{-i\\omega t}\\right] \\quad \\text{for } x > 0$$\n\n**Part (1): Deriving equations for $r$ and $t$**\n\n**Continuity of displacement at $x=0$:**\n\nAt $x=0^-$: $y(0^-, t) = \\text{Re}[(1 + r)e^{-i\\omega t}]$\nAt $x=0^+$: $y(0^+, t) = \\text{Re}[t e^{-i\\omega t}]$\n\nFor these to be equal for all $t$: $1 + r = t$ ... (i)\n\n**Equation of motion of the point mass:**\n\nThe point mass $M$ is at $x=0$. The forces on it are:\n1. Tension force from the left string: $T \\cdot \\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial x}\\bigg|_{x=0^-}$ (the string pulls the mass)\n2. Tension force from the right string: $T \\cdot \\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial x}\\bigg|_{x=0^+}$ (the string pulls the mass)\n\nWait, let me think about the signs more carefully.\n\nThe tension force on the mass from the string to the left: The string to the left of the mass has a slope $\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial x}|_{x=0^-}$. The tension force from this string on the mass points in the direction of the string. If the string slopes upward to the right (positive slope), the tension pulls the mass upward. So the force from the left string is $T \\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial x}|_{x=0^-}$.\n\nActually, let me be more careful. The tension force on the mass from the left side: The string pulls on the mass. The tension in the string is $T$. The direction of the force on the mass from the left string is along the string, pointing away from the mass toward the left. If the string at $x=0^-$ has slope $\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial x}|_{x=0^-}$, then the direction from the mass toward the left is approximately $(-1, -\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial x}|_{x=0^-})$ (unit vector pointing left along the string). So the vertical component of the tension force from the left string on the mass is $-T \\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial x}|_{x=0^-}$.\n\nWait, let me reconsider. The string extends from $-\\infty$ to $0$ on the left side. At $x=0$, the string meets the mass. The tension in the string pulls the mass. The direction of pull from the left string is along the string toward the left, i.e., in the $-x$ direction. If the string has a small slope at $x=0^-$, the unit vector pointing from the mass along the left string is approximately $(-1, -\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial x}|_{x=0^-})$ (normalized, but for small slopes, the x-component is $\\approx -1$ and y-component is $\\approx -\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial x}|_{x=0^-}$).\n\nSo the vertical component of the force from the left string on the mass is $-T\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial x}|_{x=0^-}$.\n\nSimilarly, the direction of pull from the right string is along the string toward the right, i.e., in the $+x$ direction. The unit vector pointing from the mass along the right string is approximately $(1, \\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial x}|_{x=0^+})$.\n\nSo the vertical component of the force from the right string on the mass is $+T\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial x}|_{x=0^+}$.\n\nThe spring force: The spring connects the mass to a fixed support. If the mass is displaced by $y(0,t)$, the spring force is $-K y(0,t)$ (restoring force).\n\nEquation of motion: $M \\frac{\\partial^2 y}{\\partial t^2}\\bigg|_{x=0} = T\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial x}|_{x=0^+} - T\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial x}|_{x=0^-} - Ky(0,t)$\n\nNow let's compute the slopes:\n\nFor $x < 0$: $y(x,t) = \\text{Re}[(e^{ikx} + re^{-ikx})e^{-i\\omega t}]$\n\n$\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial x} = \\text{Re}[(ik e^{ikx} - ik r e^{-ikx})e^{-i\\omega t}]$\n\nAt $x = 0^-$: $\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial x}|_{x=0^-} = \\text{Re}[(ik - ik r)e^{-i\\omega t}] = \\text{Re}[ik(1-r)e^{-i\\omega t}]$\n\nFor $x > 0$: $y(x,t) = \\text{Re}[te^{ikx}e^{-i\\omega t}]$\n\n$\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial x} = \\text{Re}[ik t e^{ikx}e^{-i\\omega t}]$\n\nAt $x = 0^+$: $\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial x}|_{x=0^+} = \\text{Re}[ik t e^{-i\\omega t}]$\n\nNow, the equation of motion in complex form (dropping Re):\n\n$M(-\\omega^2)(1+r) = ikT \\cdot t - ikT \\cdot (1-r) - K(1+r)$\n\nWait, let me be more careful. The displacement of the mass is $y(0,t) = \\text{Re}[(1+r)e^{-i\\omega t}]$.\n\nThe acceleration: $\\frac{\\partial^2 y}{\\partial t^2}|_{x=0} = \\text{Re}[(-\\omega^2)(1+r)e^{-i\\omega t}]$\n\nThe slope from the left: $\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial x}|_{x=0^-} = \\text{Re}[ik(1-r)e^{-i\\omega t}]$\n\nThe slope from the right: $\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial x}|_{x=0^+} = \\text{Re}[ikt e^{-i\\omega t}]$\n\nThe spring force: $-Ky(0,t) = \\text{Re}[-K(1+r)e^{-i\\omega t}]$\n\nEquation of motion:\n$M \\cdot \\text{Re}[(-\\omega^2)(1+r)e^{-i\\omega t}] = T \\cdot \\text{Re}[ikt e^{-i\\omega t}] - T \\cdot \\text{Re}[ik(1-r)e^{-i\\omega t}] + \\text{Re}[-K(1+r)e^{-i\\omega t}]$\n\nSince this must hold for all $t$, we can equate the complex amplitudes:\n\n$-M\\omega^2(1+r) = ikTt - ikT(1-r) - K(1+r)$\n\nExpanding:\n$-M\\omega^2(1+r) = ikTt - ikT + ikTr - K - Kr$\n\nRearranging:\n$-M\\omega^2 - M\\omega^2 r = ikTt - ikT + ikTr - K - Kr$\n\nGroup terms:\n$-M\\omega^2 + ikT + K = ikTt + ikTr + M\\omega^2 r - Kr$\n\n$-M\\omega^2 + ikT + K = ikTt + r(ikT + M\\omega^2 - K)$\n\nUsing $t = 1 + r$:\n$-M\\omega^2 + ikT + K = ikT(1+r) + r(ikT + M\\omega^2 - K)$\n\n$-M\\omega^2 + ikT + K = ikT + ikTr + ikTr + M\\omega^2 r - Kr$\n\n$-M\\omega^2 + K = 2ikTr + M\\omega^2 r - Kr$\n\n$-M\\omega^2 + K = r(2ikT + M\\omega^2 - K)$\n\n$r = \\frac{K - M\\omega^2}{2ikT + M\\omega^2 - K}$\n\nHmm, let me double-check this. Actually, let me redo this more carefully.\n\nFrom the equation of motion:\n$-M\\omega^2(1+r) = ikTt - ikT(1-r) - K(1+r)$\n\nLet me expand the right side:\n$ikTt - ikT(1-r) - K(1+r) = ikTt - ikT + ikTr - K - Kr$\n\nAnd the left side:\n$-M\\omega^2(1+r) = -M\\omega^2 - M\\omega^2 r$\n\nSo:\n$-M\\omega^2 - M\\omega^2 r = ikTt - ikT + ikTr - K - Kr$\n\nUsing $t = 1+r$:\n$-M\\omega^2 - M\\omega^2 r = ikT(1+r) - ikT + ikTr - K - Kr$\n$= ikT + ikTr - ikT + ikTr - K - Kr$\n$= 2ikTr - K - Kr$\n\nSo:\n$-M\\omega^2 - M\\omega^2 r = 2ikTr - K - Kr$\n$-M\\omega^2 + K = 2ikTr + M\\omega^2 r - Kr$\n$K - M\\omega^2 = r(2ikT + M\\omega^2 - K)$\n$r = \\frac{K - M\\omega^2}{M\\omega^2 - K + 2ikT}$\n\nNow let me define $\\eta = \\frac{K - M\\omega^2}{2kT}$.\n\nThen $K - M\\omega^2 = 2kT\\eta$ and $M\\omega^2 - K = -2kT\\eta$.\n\n$r = \\frac{2kT\\eta}{-2kT\\eta + 2ikT} = \\frac{2kT\\eta}{2kT(i - \\eta)} = \\frac{\\eta}{i - \\eta}$\n\nSo $r = \\frac{\\eta}{i - \\eta}$\n\nAnd $t = 1 + r = 1 + \\frac{\\eta}{i-\\eta} = \\frac{i-\\eta+\\eta}{i-\\eta} = \\frac{i}{i-\\eta}$\n\nLet me verify: $t = \\frac{i}{i-\\eta}$\n\nCheck: $1 + r = 1 + \\frac{\\eta}{i-\\eta} = \\frac{i-\\eta+\\eta}{i-\\eta} = \\frac{i}{i-\\eta} = t$ \u2713\n\n**Part (3): Energy reflection and transmission coefficients**\n\nThe energy flux (power per unit area) carried by a traveling wave on a string is:\n$P = T \\cdot \\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial x} \\cdot \\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial t}$\n\nFor a wave $A e^{i(kx-\\omega t)}$, the average power is:\n$P = \\frac{1}{2} T \\omega k |A|^2 = \\frac{1}{2} T \\omega^2/c \\cdot |A|^2$\n\nWait, let me be more careful. For a wave $y = \\text{Re}[Ae^{i(kx-\\omega t)}]$:\n\n$\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial x} = \\text{Re}[ikAe^{i(kx-\\omega t)}]$\n$\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial t} = \\text{Re}[-i\\omega Ae^{i(kx-\\omega t)}]$\n\nThe power: $P = T \\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial x} \\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial t}$\n\nThe average power: $\\langle P \\rangle = T \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}|ikA|^2 = \\frac{1}{2}T\\omega k |A|^2$\n\nWait, that's not right either. Let me compute more carefully.\n\n$\\langle \\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial x} \\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial t} \\rangle = \\frac{1}{2}\\text{Re}\\left[(ikA)(-i\\omega A^*)\\right] = \\frac{1}{2}\\text{Re}\\left[k\\omega|A|^2\\right] = \\frac{1}{2}k\\omega|A|^2$\n\nSo $\\langle P \\rangle = \\frac{1}{2}T\\omega k |A|^2 = \\frac{1}{2}\\mu\\omega^3 c |A|^2$... wait, let me just use $\\frac{1}{2}T\\omega k |A|^2$.\n\nActually, $T\\omega k = T \\cdot \\frac{\\omega}{c} \\cdot k = T \\cdot \\frac{\\omega k}{c} = T \\cdot \\frac{\\omega^2}{c^2} = T \\cdot \\frac{\\omega^2}{T/\\mu} = \\mu\\omega^2$.\n\nSo $\\langle P \\rangle = \\frac{1}{2}\\mu\\omega^2 |A|^2$.\n\nNow, the incident power (amplitude 1):\n$P_{\\text{inc}} = \\frac{1}{2}T\\omega k \\cdot 1 = \\frac{1}{2}\\mu\\omega^2$\n\nThe reflected power (amplitude $r$):\n$P_{\\text{ref}} = \\frac{1}{2}T\\omega k |r|^2$\n\nThe transmitted power (amplitude $t$):\n$P_{\\text{trans}} = \\frac{1}{2}T\\omega k |t|^2$\n\nNote: The transmitted wave is traveling in the $+x$ direction, so its power flows to the right. The reflected wave is traveling in the $-x$ direction, so its power flows to the left. The incident wave is traveling in the $+x$ direction, so its power flows to the right.\n\nThe reflection coefficient:\n$R = \\frac{P_{\\text{ref}}}{P_{\\text{inc}}} = |r|^2$\n\nThe transmission coefficient:\n$\\mathcal{T} = \\frac{P_{\\text{trans}}}{P_{\\text{inc}}} = |t|^2$\n\nNow let's compute these:\n\n$|r|^2 = \\left|\\frac{\\eta}{i-\\eta}\\right|^2 = \\frac{\\eta^2}{\\eta^2 + 1}$\n\n$|t|^2 = \\left|\\frac{i}{i-\\eta}\\right|^2 = \\frac{1}{\\eta^2 + 1}$\n\nSo $R + \\mathcal{T} = \\frac{\\eta^2}{\\eta^2+1} + \\frac{1}{\\eta^2+1} = 1$ \u2713\n\n**Part (4): Perfect transmission**\n\nPerfect transmission occurs when $R = 0$, i.e., when $r = 0$.\n\n$r = \\frac{\\eta}{i-\\eta} = 0$ when $\\eta = 0$.\n\n$\\eta = 0$ means $K - M\\omega^2 = 0$, i.e., $\\omega = \\sqrt{K/M}$.\n\nPhysical reason: At this frequency, the mass-spring system is at resonance. The spring force exactly balances the inertial force of the mass for any oscillation, so the point mass \"disappears\" \u2014 it doesn't reflect the wave. The mass oscillates with the wave but the net force from the mass on the string is zero (the spring provides exactly the force needed to accelerate the mass), so the wave sees no discontinuity.\n\nWait, let me think about this more carefully. At $\\omega = \\sqrt{K/M}$, the equation of motion of the mass becomes:\n$M\\ddot{y} + Ky = 0$ (without the string forces)\n\nSo the mass is in natural oscillation. The spring force $-Ky$ provides exactly the force needed to produce the acceleration $M\\ddot{y} = -Ky$ (since $\\ddot{y} = -\\omega^2 y = -(K/M)y$). So the net force from the string on the mass is zero, meaning the mass doesn't perturb the string at all, and the wave passes through as if the mass isn't there.\n\nActually, I should be more precise. The equation of motion is:\n$M\\ddot{y} = T\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial x}|_{0^+} - T\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial x}|_{0^-} - Ky$\n\nAt $\\omega = \\sqrt{K/M}$: $-M\\omega^2(1+r) = -K(1+r)$ (since $M\\omega^2 = K$)\n\nSo: $-K(1+r) = ikTt - ikT(1-r) - K(1+r)$\n\nThis gives: $ikTt = ikT(1-r)$, so $t = 1-r$.\n\nCombined with $t = 1+r$: $1-r = 1+r$, so $r = 0$ and $t = 1$.\n\nSo the physical explanation is that at the resonant frequency of the mass-spring system ($\\omega = \\sqrt{K/M}$), the spring force exactly provides the force needed to oscillate the mass, so the mass doesn't exert any net force on the string. The wave passes through undisturbed.\n\nLet me also double-check the sign convention for the slope equation. Let me redo the force analysis.\n\nThe mass at $x=0$ is pulled by:\n- The left portion of the string (from $-\\infty$ to $0$): The tension force points along the string away from the mass, i.e., to the left. The vertical component is $-T\\sin\\theta_{\\text{left}} \\approx -T\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial x}|_{x=0^-}$ where $\\theta_{\\text{left}}$ is the angle the string makes with the horizontal at $x=0^-$.\n\nWait, I need to be more careful. If the string at $x=0^-$ has a positive slope (going up as $x$ increases), then the string to the left of the mass is going downward as we move left. The tension pulls the mass in the direction the string goes, which is to the left and downward. So the vertical component is negative: $-T\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial x}|_{x=0^-}$.\n\nHmm, actually let me think about this differently. Consider a small element of string at position $x$. The tension forces on it from the left and right neighbors are:\n- From the right: $T$ in the direction of increasing $x$ along the string, vertical component $\\approx T\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial x}$\n- From the left: $T$ in the direction of decreasing $x$ along the string, vertical component $\\approx -T\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial x}$\n\nWait, that's for a uniform string. Let me think about the mass specifically.\n\nThe mass is at $x=0$. The string to its left ($x<0$) pulls on it. The tension in the string is $T$. The direction of the pull is along the string, pointing away from the mass toward the left. If the string has slope $\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial x}|_{x=0^-}$ at $x=0^-$, then moving from the mass toward the left (decreasing $x$), the string goes in the direction $(-1, -\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial x}|_{x=0^-})$ (to first order). So the vertical component of the tension force from the left string is $-T\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial x}|_{x=0^-}$.\n\nThe string to its right ($x>0$) pulls on it. Moving from the mass toward the right (increasing $x$), the string goes in the direction $(1, \\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial x}|_{x=0^+})$. So the vertical component of the tension force from the right string is $+T\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial x}|_{x=0^+}$.\n\nThe spring: If the mass is displaced upward by $y$, the spring (attached to a fixed support below) pulls it downward with force $-Ky$.\n\nSo the equation of motion is:\n$M\\ddot{y}(0,t) = T\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial x}|_{x=0^+} - T\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial x}|_{x=0^-} - Ky(0,t)$\n\nThis is what I had before. Good.\n\nNow let me verify with the complex amplitudes:\n\n$-M\\omega^2 \\cdot (1+r) = T \\cdot ik \\cdot t - T \\cdot ik \\cdot (1-r) - K(1+r)$\n\n$-M\\omega^2(1+r) = ikTt - ikT(1-r) - K(1+r)$\n\nThis confirms what I had. Let me solve this system again more carefully.\n\nFrom continuity: $t = 1 + r$ ... (1)\n\nFrom EOM: $-M\\omega^2(1+r) = ikTt - ikT(1-r) - K(1+r)$ ... (2)\n\nSubstituting (1) into (2):\n$-M\\omega^2(1+r) = ikT(1+r) - ikT(1-r) - K(1+r)$\n\n$-M\\omega^2(1+r) = ikT(1+r - 1 + r) - K(1+r)$\n\n$-M\\omega^2(1+r) = ikT \\cdot 2r - K(1+r)$\n\n$-M\\omega^2(1+r) + K(1+r) = 2ikTr$\n\n$(K - M\\omega^2)(1+r) = 2ikTr$\n\n$(K - M\\omega^2) + (K - M\\omega^2)r = 2ikTr$\n\n$(K - M\\omega^2) = 2ikTr - (K - M\\omega^2)r$\n\n$(K - M\\omega^2) = r[2ikT - (K - M\\omega^2)]$\n\n$r = \\frac{K - M\\omega^2}{2ikT - K + M\\omega^2} = \\frac{K - M\\omega^2}{(M\\omega^2 - K) + 2ikT}$\n\nNow with $\\eta = \\frac{K - M\\omega^2}{2kT}$:\n\nNumerator: $K - M\\omega^2 = 2kT\\eta$\nDenominator: $(M\\omega^2 - K) + 2ikT = -2kT\\eta + 2ikT = 2kT(i - \\eta)$\n\nSo: $r = \\frac{2kT\\eta}{2kT(i-\\eta)} = \\frac{\\eta}{i-\\eta}$\n\nAnd $t = 1 + r = \\frac{i-\\eta+\\eta}{i-\\eta} = \\frac{i}{i-\\eta}$\n\nThis confirms my earlier results.\n\nFor part (3):\n$R = |r|^2 = \\frac{\\eta^2}{(i-\\eta)(-i-\\eta)} = \\frac{\\eta^2}{\\eta^2+1}$\n\n$\\mathcal{T} = |t|^2 = \\frac{i(-i)}{(i-\\eta)(-i-\\eta)} = \\frac{1}{\\eta^2+1}$\n\n$R + \\mathcal{T} = \\frac{\\eta^2+1}{\\eta^2+1} = 1$ \u2713\n\nEverything checks out. Let me write up the solution.\n"
